# Explore the Geometry of Search

A binary search can be viewed as a tree of decisions. This notebook begins with an intuitive designer, then reveals exact counting, asymptotic dimension, and finally the boundary between geometry and computational cost. For background, see [box-counting dimension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box-counting_dimension), [binary trees](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_tree), and [entropy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_(information_theory)).

## 1. Build a periodic search profile

At a **free** level both choices survive; at a constrained level only one does. Choose the density of free levels and watch the dimension change.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the definition and ambient bound</summary>

If $N(n)$ is the number of viable prefixes at depth $n$, define $d_n=\log_2N(n)/n$ and $D=\limsup d_n$. Since $N(n)\le 2^n$, every binary profile satisfies $D\le1$.

</details>

## 2. Count instead of enumerating

There is no need to generate exponentially many words. Quotient–remainder decomposition counts free levels directly.

{{algorithm:0}}

Try the complete executable demonstration:

{{demo:0}}

## 3. Exact returns at complete periods

Partial periods create a small oscillation. Every complete period lands exactly on the limiting dimension.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the periodic density proof</summary>

Write $n=qk+r$. If $p$ residues are free, then the free count is $pk+a(r)$, where $a(r)$ is bounded. Hence $F(n)/n\to p/q$. When $r=0$, $F(qk)=pk$, so the finite estimate is exactly $p/q$.

</details>

## 4. Survey the rational landscape

Every rational $p/q$ in $[0,1]$ is created by making $p$ of each $q$ levels free.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

## 5. Geometry is not search time

Now hold the complete geometry fixed and alter a separately attached shortest-length label.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the non-determination argument</summary>

First construct a periodic profile with dimension $p/q$ and exact estimate $p/q$ at depth $qk$. Then pair that unchanged profile with any natural number $L$. Because no terminal-placement or exploration-policy law links $L$ to the profile, all geometric observations stay fixed while $L$ varies.

</details>

## 6. Compare exponential growth rates

The slope of $\log_2N(n)$ is the dimension. This final plot compares several profiles.

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

The central takeaway is that dimension precisely measures exponential abundance of viable prefixes, while search time additionally requires a policy, failure costs, and terminal structure.

